#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065385 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a $10\\,\\textrm{ft}$ ladder that is leaning against the wall. A man is pushing the bottom of the ladder towards the wall at a rate of $1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s}.$ How fast is the top of the ladder sliding up the wall when the bottom is at a distance of $8\\,\\textrm{ft}$ from the wall?\n\nTo solve this and similar problems, we can follow the four steps below.\n\n**Step 1:** Draw a diagram. \n\n![](images/42/42fce00bf688c28024f568b75e60335e40e81dbc266abf64adde28efe235a9f3.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the bottom of the ladder and the wall,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the ladder.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle.\n\n** Step 2:** Use the Pythagorean theorem to find the missing side.\n\nAt the particular moment in question we have $x = 8\\textrm{ ft}$ and $z=10\\textrm{ ft}.$ So, we can find $y$ at this moment using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {8}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={10}^{2} \\\\ 64+{y}^{2} & =100 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n** Step 3:** Identify the variables that change with time.\n\n- The distance of the ladder from the wall ($x$) changes with time at a rate of $1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s},$ which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = -1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s}.\n$$\n\nThe rate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ is the velocity of the bottom of the ladder. It is negative because the ladder moves towards the wall (so $x$ becomes smaller with time).\n\n- The length of the ladder ($z$) is fixed and doesn't change with time, so\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 0.\n$$\n\n- The distance of the top of the ladder from the ground ($y$) changes with time, and it's what we are looking for:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = \\textrm{?}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Take the derivative with respect to time and plug in the known values.\n\nTo find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t},$ we differentiate both sides of $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with respect to $t,$ using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}-2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values and solve for the unknown rate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(6)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(10)(0)-2(8)(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{0+16}{12} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the ladder slides up the wall at a rate of approx $1.33 \\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s}$ when the bottom is at a distance of $8\\,\\textrm{ft}$ from the wall." :tutorial/id #uuid "519a6401-a713-563f-bb3e-49181cd7bc50" :tutorial/math-academy-id 492 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
